# Movie collector project conventions

Personal TMDB-backed movie list site. Keep changes small, match existing patterns, and prefer plain Node/HTML/CSS over new libraries or bundlers.

## Hard constraints

These are deliberate design decisions, not gaps to fill:

- **No edit layer.** You search and add. TMDB's titles, overviews, and posters are what render. Do not add an override store, edit dialog, or compaction logic. All movie metadata comes from D1/TMDB at runtime.
- **Nothing local writes user data.** The browser is the only writer of lists, ratings, and preferences, into `localStorage` and the CineQueue account API. `server.js` is a read-only static file server and exposes no API. No local script writes site assets.
- **Only ids are persisted.** The user state holds lists of TMDB ids in display order, plus a `statuses` map of id → `{ status, at }` for sync, a `ratings` map, and an `addedAt` map of id → ISO stamp for when the movie first entered the collection. Movie records are never duplicated into user state; they are rehydrated by id from the account D1 batch cache (`POST /api/movies/batch`), then the Cache API wrapper, then per-id TMDB.
- **Metadata from D1, posters from TMDB's CDN.** `hydrateMovies` in `js/app/03-tmdb-client.js` resolves ids from the browser movie cache first, then the account batch endpoint, then per-id TMDB. Every layer that resolves a record writes it back to the cache — a hydration path that fetches without caching makes repeat visits re-fetch the whole collection. Stale cached records render immediately and refresh by joining the batch already going out; do not reintroduce per-id background revalidation. Poster images are always `image.tmdb.org` URLs built by `buildImageUrl`: unauthenticated, direct from the browser, never through the Worker or Netlify. Do not reintroduce a committed poster cache, a scraper, or a poster proxy — the API budget does not cover images, so there is nothing to save.
- **Two record shapes, two readers.** The browser cache, D1, and the batch response all hold normalized records (camelCase), read with `appTmdb.parseStoredMovieRecord`. Raw TMDB payloads (snake_case) go through `normalizeMovie`. `normalizeMovie` is not idempotent — on its own output every field blanks while `genres` stays an array, so `isDetailedMovieRecord` still passes and the corruption is silent. Never feed a stored record back through `normalizeMovie`, and bump `TMDB_CACHE_NAME` if the stored shape changes.
- **Preset lists stay fixed; custom lists are optional tags.** Watched and Watchlist are statuses defined by `PRESET_LISTS` in `scripts/lib/lists.js`. Up to 10 user-defined custom lists live in `customLists` / `customListTombstones` (`scripts/lib/custom-lists.js`, `scripts/lib/custom-list-merge.js`) and are independent of preset membership. Do not fold custom lists into `PRESET_LISTS` or add a third preset list.
- **One preset invariant, enforced on read.** Watchlist is disjoint from Watched. `assignMovieToList` and `removeMovieFromList` in `scripts/lib/lists.js` are the only ways to change preset membership, and `normalizeLists` repairs any stored payload that violates the rule. Never add or remove ids from a preset `movieIds` array directly. Custom list membership uses `addMovieToCustomList` / `removeMovieFromCustomList` only.
- **Every membership change records a status.** Adding, marking watched, and removing must call `recordMovieStatus` so `scripts/lib/sync-merge.js` has a per-movie stamp to merge on. Skipping it lets a stale tab out-vote the change. `removed` is a sync record only — it never appears in a `movieIds` array and is never rendered or fetched.
- **Sync never blind-writes.** Saving to the account API reads remote state first, merges, then writes, all through one serialized promise chain. A payload that cannot be read is never overwritten. Merges compare per-movie stamps, not the payload's `updatedAt`, and a tie keeps the movie. Do not reintroduce whole-payload last-write-wins.
- **Account-gated TMDB.** `hasTmdbAccess()` is `accountSyncEnabled()`. TMDB traffic goes through the Worker proxy (`GET /api/tmdb`) with the account bearer token. The server holds `TMDB_READ_TOKEN`; users never paste a personal TMDB token in Settings. Localhost uses the same rules as production.
- **Account-only storage mode.** `storageMode` is always `"account"`. Legacy `"local"` / `"gist"` values normalize on read. No Gist sync, local-only mode, or hosted unlock UI. Login/signup pulls remote lists only — no push of local lists on connect; CSV export/import is the migration path.
- **Routing is hash-only.** Deep links: `#movie/{id}`, `#lists`, `#lists/{customId}`. No path routes, no SPA fallback, no `_redirects`.
- **Search is add-only.** The search box queries TMDB to find movies to add; it does not filter your list.
- **Watched-only display sort.** Optional sort modes apply on the Watched tab and on custom list detail grids; they never rewrite stored order. Watchlist always uses custom order. Custom order is required for drag reorder on Watchlist.

## Layout

| Path | Role |
|------|------|
| `index.html` | UI shell (loads `js/app-bundle.js`) |
| `js/app/` | App source partials; `npm run bundle` → `js/app-bundle.js` |
| `js/app/00-*.js` | **Generated** from `scripts/lib/` — never hand-edit |
| `css/` | All styles — no inline `<style>` in `index.html` |
| `scripts/lib/` | Pure CommonJS domain logic, one concern per file |
| `test/` | Node tests (`npm test`) |
| `server.js` | Read-only static server (`npm run serve`) |
| `build.js` → `build/` | Static deploy output (`npm run build`) |

`build.js` ships no `data/` directory. A new site asset needs an explicit copy step in `build()`, or the deployed site will not see it.

## JavaScript

Vanilla JS. **CommonJS** in `scripts/` and `test/`. No bundler, no TypeScript, no frameworks.

`js/app/` partials share **one IIFE scope**, opened by `01-config-dom-state.js` and closed by `08-init.js`. Top-level `const`/`let`/`function` in one partial is visible to every later partial. Load order is the `PARTS` array in [`scripts/bundle-app-js.js`](../../scripts/bundle-app-js.js).

Numeric prefixes signal layering:

| Prefix | Role |
|--------|------|
| `00-*` | Generated namespaces from `scripts/lib/` |
| `01-*` | Config, DOM refs, mutable state (opens the IIFE) |
| `03-*` | User state and the TMDB client |
| `04-*` | Search |
| `05-*` | Render |
| `06-*` | Dialogs |
| `07-*` | Drag reorder |
| `08-*` | Event wiring and startup (closes the IIFE) |

**Domain logic belongs in `scripts/lib/`** as pure CommonJS, tested under `test/`, then mirrored into `js/app/00-*.js` by `npm run bundle`. Add the module to [`scripts/app-sync-config.js`](../../scripts/app-sync-config.js) with its export list. Do not leave behavior-only logic untested in `js/app/` alone.

For a lib module that depends on another, use runtime detection so it works in both environments:

```js
function getLists() {
  if (typeof appLists !== "undefined") {
    return appLists;
  }
  return require("./lists");
}
```

After editing anything under `scripts/lib/` or `js/app/`, run `npm run bundle`.

## CSS

Styles live in `css/`, linked from `index.html` in this order (load order matters):

1. `variables.css` — `:root` custom properties
2. `base.css` — reset, body, layout primitives
3. `header.css` — header, search, toolbar
4. `settings.css` — settings and about dialogs, footer bar
5. `cards.css` — grid, cards, skeletons, list view, drag states
6. `movie-detail.css` — detail overlay
7. `mobile.css` — `@media (max-width: 640px)` overrides

Canonical order: `VIEWER_CSS_FILES` in [`scripts/css-manifest.js`](../../scripts/css-manifest.js). Keep the `index.html` link tags in sync. Put new styles in the most specific existing file; add a file only for a distinct UI surface.

## Tests

**Always add or update tests** when changing `scripts/lib/` logic, data transforms, or non-trivial state machines. Skip only for purely visual/CSS tweaks or copy edits.

- Runner: Node built-in `node:test` + `node:assert/strict` — no new test frameworks
- Command: `npm test`
- Location: `test/<topic>.test.js`
- Tests `require()` the `scripts/lib/` module directly, never the generated partial

## Security

- The account session token lives in `localStorage` and is **never** written into the synced list doc. The TMDB read token lives only on the Worker as `TMDB_READ_TOKEN`.
- Never commit an API key. There is no key in the repo and there must not be one. `npm run letterboxd-import` reads `TMDB_READ_TOKEN` from the environment or the gitignored `.env`, and nothing writes it into a committed file.
- Only the query string is user-derived in TMDB URLs; hosts are hardcoded constants. Validate `poster_path` before interpolating it into an image URL.
- `poster_path` from TMDB is validated by `isValidImagePath` before it is interpolated into an image URL, and the size must be in `ALLOWED_IMAGE_SIZES`. Both host constants are hardcoded, so no API response can redirect an image request.
- Every network call sets an explicit timeout.

## General editing principles

- Minimize diff scope; don't refactor unrelated code
- Match naming, formatting, and abstractions of surrounding files
- Comments only for non-obvious logic
- Update `README.md` when scripts, layout, or major features change
- Run `npm test` and `npm run bundle` before finishing
